var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API reference","text":"Pages = [\"api.md\"]\nDepth = 2","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Module","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Module"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Gray-code","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Gray code"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Gates","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Gates"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Constructors","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Constructors"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Circuits","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Circuits"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Decompositions","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Decompositions"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.QuantumCircuits","page":"API reference","text":"QuantumCircuits\n\nA small, dependency-free Julia package for building quantum circuits and decomposing structured unitaries into elementary gates.\n\nThe organising idea of the first release is Gray coding: ordering the computational basis so that consecutive states differ in exactly one bit turns the CNOT ladders in a decomposition into a single CNOT per step.  See docs/graycode.md.\n\nConventions\n\nQubit 1 is the most significant bit of a basis index (|q1 q2 … qn⟩).\ncontrols[1] is the most significant bit of a multiplexor's branch index.\nRotations are R(θ) = exp(-i θ P / 2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.QuantumCircuits"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.gray","page":"API reference","text":"gray(i) -> Int\n\nStandard reflected binary Gray code of i: i ⊻ (i >> 1).\n\ngray is a bijection on 0:2^n-1 for every n, and gray(i) and gray(i+1) always differ in exactly one bit.\n\njulia> gray.(0:7)\n8-element Vector{Int64}:\n 0\n 1\n 3\n 2\n 6\n 7\n 5\n 4\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.gray"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.ungray","page":"API reference","text":"ungray(g) -> Int\n\nInverse of gray: recovers i from gray(i) by prefix-XOR.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.ungray"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.graycode","page":"API reference","text":"graycode(n) -> Vector{Int}\n\nThe full n-bit Gray code sequence, gray.(0:2^n-1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.graycode"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.gray_flip_position","page":"API reference","text":"gray_flip_position(i) -> Int\n\nIndex (LSB = 0) of the single bit that changes between gray(i-1) and gray(i).\n\nThis is just trailing_zeros(i), which is the whole reason the Gray-code CNOT ladder is cheap to generate: the control qubit for step i is a ctz away.\n\n\n\n\n\ngray_flip_position(i, k) -> Int\n\nCyclic version over k bits.  For i == 2^k the sequence wraps from gray(2^k - 1) back to gray(0) == 0, which flips the most significant bit, so this returns k-1.  Closing the cycle is what makes a multiplexor's CNOTs cancel out to the identity on the control register.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.gray_flip_position"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.gray_flip_positions","page":"API reference","text":"gray_flip_positions(k) -> Vector{Int}\n\nThe 2^k bit positions flipped by one full cyclic walk of the k-bit Gray code.  cumulative XOR of 1 << p over this list reproduces gray(1), gray(2), …, gray(2^k-1), 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.gray_flip_positions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.gray_adjacent","page":"API reference","text":"gray_adjacent(a, b) -> Bool\n\ntrue when a and b differ in exactly one bit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.gray_adjacent"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.gray_walk","page":"API reference","text":"gray_walk(a, b) -> Vector{Int}\n\nBit positions (LSB = 0) to flip, one at a time, to walk from basis state a to basis state b.  Each intermediate state is Gray-adjacent to the previous one. Used by multi-controlled-gate constructions that need to visit a set of computational basis states with one X/CNOT per hop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.gray_walk"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.hamming","page":"API reference","text":"hamming(a, b) -> Int\n\nHamming distance between two non-negative integers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.hamming"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.parity","page":"API reference","text":"parity(a, b) -> Int\n\nBitwise dot product a · b mod 2, i.e. the parity of a & b.\n\nThe characters of Z_2^k are (-1)^parity(a,b); the Walsh–Hadamard transform behind every multiplexed rotation is built from exactly this.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.parity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.bits","page":"API reference","text":"bits(x, n) -> String\n\nn-bit binary string of x, most significant bit first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.bits"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.Gate","page":"API reference","text":"Gate(name, params, mat)\n\nA named unitary.  mat is 2^k × 2^k for a k-qubit gate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.Gate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.nqubits","page":"API reference","text":"nqubits(g::Gate)\nnqubits(c::Circuit)\n\nNumber of qubits a gate acts on, or the width of a circuit's register.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.nqubits"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.matrix","page":"API reference","text":"matrix(g::Gate)\nmatrix(c::Circuit)\n\nThe dense unitary of a gate, or of a whole circuit.\n\n\n\n\n\nmatrix(c) -> Matrix{ComplexF64}\n\nThe full 2^n × 2^n unitary of the circuit (column j is the circuit applied to basis state j-1).  Exponential in n — for inspection and testing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.label","page":"API reference","text":"label(g) -> String\n\nShort display label, e.g. \"RZ(1.57)\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.label"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.controlled","page":"API reference","text":"controlled(g, n=1) -> Gate\n\nAdd n control qubits to g.  In the resulting matrix the controls are the most significant bits, so controlled(X()) is CNOT().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.controlled"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.Id","page":"API reference","text":"Id()\n\nThe one-qubit identity, [1 0; 0 1].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.Id"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.X","page":"API reference","text":"X()\n\nPauli-X (bit flip), [0 1; 1 0].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.X"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.Y","page":"API reference","text":"Y()\n\nPauli-Y, [0 -im; im 0].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.Y"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.Z","page":"API reference","text":"Z()\n\nPauli-Z (phase flip), [1 0; 0 -1].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.Z"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.H","page":"API reference","text":"H()\n\nHadamard, [1 1; 1 -1]/√2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.H"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.S","page":"API reference","text":"S()\n\nPhase gate diag(1, im); S == PHASE(π/2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.S"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.Sdg","page":"API reference","text":"Sdg()\n\nAdjoint of S, diag(1, -im).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.Sdg"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.T","page":"API reference","text":"T()\n\nπ/8 gate diag(1, exp(iπ/4)); T^2 == S.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.T"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.Tdg","page":"API reference","text":"Tdg()\n\nAdjoint of T, diag(1, exp(-iπ/4)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.Tdg"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.RX","page":"API reference","text":"RX(θ)\n\nRotation exp(-i θ X / 2) about the x-axis.  See also RY, RZ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.RX"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.RY","page":"API reference","text":"RY(θ)\n\nRotation exp(-i θ Y / 2) about the y-axis.\n\nX RY(θ) X == RY(-θ) — the sign flip that the Gray-code multiplexor (multiplexed_rotation!) exploits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.RY"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.RZ","page":"API reference","text":"RZ(θ)\n\nRotation exp(-i θ Z / 2) = diag(exp(-iθ/2), exp(iθ/2)) about the z-axis.\n\nX RZ(θ) X == RZ(-θ) — the sign flip that the Gray-code multiplexor (multiplexed_rotation!) exploits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.RZ"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.PHASE","page":"API reference","text":"PHASE(λ)\n\ndiag(1, exp(im*λ)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.PHASE"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.CNOT","page":"API reference","text":"CNOT()\n\nControlled-NOT.  Used as push!(c, CNOT(), control, target); the first wire is the control (the most significant bit of the matrix index).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.CNOT"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.CZ","page":"API reference","text":"CZ()\n\nControlled-Z, diag(1, 1, 1, -1).  Symmetric in its two wires.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.CZ"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.SWAP","page":"API reference","text":"SWAP()\n\nExchange the states of two qubits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.SWAP"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.Circuit","page":"API reference","text":"Circuit(n)\n\nAn n-qubit circuit: an ordered list of Instructions plus an accumulated global_phase (in radians).  Global phase is unobservable on its own but is tracked so that matrix and statevector are exact rather than correct-up-to-phase, which makes tests much sharper.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.Circuit"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.Instruction","page":"API reference","text":"Instruction(gate, qubits)\n\nA gate together with the (1-based) wires it acts on.  qubits[1] maps to the most significant index bit of gate.mat.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.Instruction"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.push!-Tuple{Circuit, Gate, Vararg{Integer}}","page":"API reference","text":"push!(c, gate, qubits...)\n\nAppend gate acting on qubits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.push!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.append!-Tuple{Circuit, Circuit}","page":"API reference","text":"append!(c, other)\n\nAppend every instruction of other (and its global phase) onto c.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.append!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.statevector","page":"API reference","text":"statevector(c, ψ0 = |0…0⟩) -> Vector{ComplexF64}\n\nRun the circuit on ψ0 and return the resulting statevector, including the tracked global phase.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.statevector"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.zero_state","page":"API reference","text":"zero_state(n) -> Vector{ComplexF64}\n\nThe |0…0⟩ statevector on n qubits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.zero_state"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.apply!","page":"API reference","text":"apply!(ψ, U, qubits, n) -> ψ\n\nApply the 2^k × 2^k matrix U to wires qubits of the n-qubit statevector ψ, in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.apply!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.draw","page":"API reference","text":"draw([io], c)\n\nPrint an ASCII diagram of the circuit, one column per instruction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.draw"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.count_gates","page":"API reference","text":"count_gates(c, name) -> Int\n\nNumber of instructions whose gate has the given name, e.g. count_gates(c, :CNOT).  Handy for checking synthesis cost.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.count_gates"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.count_cnots","page":"API reference","text":"count_cnots(c) -> Int\n\nNumber of CNOTs in the circuit — the usual proxy for cost on hardware, and the figure of merit every decomposition in this package is trying to minimise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.count_cnots"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.multiplex_angles","page":"API reference","text":"multiplex_angles(α) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nSolve α = M θ where M[j+1, i] = (-1)^(gray(i-1) · j), i.e. return the rotation angles θ that a Gray-code multiplexor must apply in order to realise the branch angles α.\n\nM is a column-permuted Walsh–Hadamard matrix, so M Mᵀ = 2^k I and the inverse is just the transpose over 2^k:\n\nθ_i = 2^{-k} Σ_j (-1)^{gray(i-1) · j} α_j\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.multiplex_angles"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.multiplex_matrix","page":"API reference","text":"multiplex_matrix(k) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nThe 2^k × 2^k transform M[j+1, i] = (-1)^(gray(i-1) · j) used by multiplex_angles.  Exposed for inspection/tests; the solver never builds it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.multiplex_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.multiplexed_rotation!","page":"API reference","text":"multiplexed_rotation!(c, kind, α, controls, target) -> c\n\nAppend a uniformly controlled :RY or :RZ rotation to c: when the control register is in state |j⟩, R(α[j+1]) is applied to target.\n\ncontrols[1] is the most significant bit of j, matching the ket reading order used everywhere else in this package.\n\nCosts exactly 2^k rotations and 2^k CNOTs for k = length(controls) (k == 0 degenerates to a single rotation, no CNOTs).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.multiplexed_rotation!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.multiplexed_ry","page":"API reference","text":"multiplexed_ry(α, controls, target; n)\n\nStandalone circuit for a uniformly controlled RY on n qubits (default: the smallest register that fits the given wires).  See multiplexed_rotation!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.multiplexed_ry"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.multiplexed_rz","page":"API reference","text":"multiplexed_rz(α, controls, target; n)\n\nStandalone circuit for a uniformly controlled RZ on n qubits (default: the smallest register that fits the given wires).  See multiplexed_rotation!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.multiplexed_rz"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.diagonal","page":"API reference","text":"diagonal(φ) -> Circuit\n\nCircuit for the diagonal unitary diag(exp(im .* φ)) on log2(length(φ)) qubits, using 2^n - 2 CNOTs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.diagonal"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.diagonal!","page":"API reference","text":"diagonal!(c, φ, qubits) -> global_phase\n\nAppend a circuit implementing diag(exp(im .* φ)) on qubits, returning the leftover global phase (the caller decides what to do with it; the diagonal wrapper folds it into the circuit).\n\nRecursive peeling: split the index j = (j', b) on the last qubit.  The difference of the two phases in each pair is a uniformly controlled RZ (one Gray-code multiplexor), the mean is a diagonal on one fewer qubit.\n\nCNOTs(n) = CNOTs(n-1) + 2^(n-1)   ⟹   CNOTs(n) = 2^n - 2\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.diagonal!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#QuantumCircuits.prepare_state","page":"API reference","text":"prepare_state(a) -> Circuit\n\nMöttönen-style state preparation: a circuit mapping |0…0⟩ to the (normalised) amplitude vector a.\n\nTwo Gray-code passes:\n\na cascade of uniformly controlled RY rotations, one level per qubit, distributing the magnitudes down the binary tree of amplitudes;\na diagonal unitary stamping on the phases.\n\nTotal CNOT count: (2^n - 2) from the RY cascade plus (2^n - 2) from the diagonal, i.e. 2^{n+1} - 4 — versus the O(n 2^n) you would pay with multi-controlled rotations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"QuantumCircuits.prepare_state"},{"category":"section","location":"graycode/#Gray-coding-and-efficient-quantum-circuit-decomposition","page":"Gray coding","text":"","title":"Gray coding and efficient quantum circuit decomposition"},{"category":"section","location":"graycode/#1.-The-code","page":"Gray coding","text":"The reflected binary Gray code is the map\n\ngray(i) = i ⊻ (i >> 1)\n\nIt is a bijection on 0 … 2ⁿ-1, and its defining property is that gray(i) and gray(i+1) differ in exactly one bit. For n = 3:\n\ni binary gray(i) bit flipped from the previous row\n0 000 000 —\n1 001 001 0\n2 010 011 1\n3 011 010 0\n4 100 110 2\n5 101 111 0\n6 110 101 1\n7 111 100 0\n\nTwo facts do all the work downstream:\n\nWhich bit flips is free to compute. Going from gray(i-1) to gray(i) flips bit trailing_zeros(i) — that is gray_flip_position(i). No search, no table.\nThe walk closes into a cycle. gray(2ⁿ-1) = 100…0 is one bit away from gray(0) = 0, so a full cyclic walk flips every bit an even number of times. gray_flip_position(i, k) returns k-1 for the wrap-around step, and gray_flip_positions(k) gives the whole cycle.\n\nFact 1 makes the synthesis loop O(1) per gate. Fact 2 is what makes the resulting circuit unitary-correct rather than merely close: everything the construction scribbles on the control register is erased by the time the walk returns to the start.","title":"1. The code"},{"category":"section","location":"graycode/#2.-Why-a-quantum-compiler-cares","page":"Gray coding","text":"Almost every interesting unitary is specified as a table of 2ⁿ things — one rotation angle per control branch, one phase per basis state, one amplitude per basis state. A structurally naive compilation treats those 2ⁿ entries as 2ⁿ independent multi-controlled gates. Each multi-controlled gate costs O(n) CNOTs (or needs ancillas), so you pay O(n · 2ⁿ).\n\nBut the entries are not independent — they are indexed by bitstrings, and you get to choose the order in which you process them. Choose Gray-code order and consecutive entries differ in one bit, so the transition between them costs a single CNOT instead of an uncompute/recompute ladder. That is the entire trick, and it recurs everywhere:\n\nTask naive Gray-code\nUniformly controlled rotation, k controls O(k·2ᵏ) CNOTs 2ᵏ CNOTs\nDiagonal unitary on n qubits O(n·2ⁿ) 2ⁿ - 2\nArbitrary state preparation O(n·2ⁿ) 2ⁿ⁺¹ - 4\nMulti-controlled U (Barenco et al. Lemma 7.5) — O(n²), ancilla-free\nTrotterised Pauli-string exponentials ladder per term one CNOT per term\n\nOn hardware where two-qubit gates dominate both the error budget and the runtime, dropping the factor of n and every ancilla is the whole ballgame.","title":"2. Why a quantum compiler cares"},{"category":"section","location":"graycode/#3.-The-uniformly-controlled-rotation","page":"Gray coding","text":"The central primitive, sometimes called a multiplexed rotation or multiplexor:\n\nUCR(α) = Σ_j |j⟩⟨j| ⊗ R(α_j),     j = 0 … 2ᵏ-1\n\nwith k control qubits and one target: \"if the controls read j, rotate the target by α_j\". R is RZ or RY.","title":"3. The uniformly controlled rotation"},{"category":"section","location":"graycode/#The-construction","page":"Gray coding","text":"Alternate rotations on the target with CNOTs into the target, 2ᵏ of each:\n\nR(θ₁) ─ CNOT(c₁) ─ R(θ₂) ─ CNOT(c₂) ─ … ─ R(θ_{2ᵏ}) ─ CNOT(c_{2ᵏ})\n\nwhere cᵢ is the control qubit named by the Gray-code flip position at step i. No multi-qubit control anywhere.","title":"The construction"},{"category":"section","location":"graycode/#Why-it-works","page":"Gray coding","text":"Every gate is diagonal on the control register, so fix the controls in a basis state |j⟩ and ask what happens to the target. Each CNOT is then just X^b, with b the value of the relevant bit of j. The key identity is\n\nX R(θ) X = R(-θ)          for R ∈ {RX, RY, RZ}\n\nso a rotation sandwiched between an odd number of Xs comes back with its angle negated. Before step i, the target has been hit by the Xs from control bits c₁ … c_{i-1}, so the sign of θᵢ is (-1) to the parity of those bits of j. The mask of bits toggled so far is exactly the accumulated XOR of the flip masks, which is the definition of the Gray code:\n\nmask after i-1 steps = gray(i-1)\n\nAll the rotations commute (same axis), so they simply add:\n\nα_j = Σᵢ (-1)^(gray(i-1) · j) · θᵢ\n\nwhere · is the bitwise dot product mod 2 (parity in this package). And by fact 2, the mask after all 2ᵏ steps is 0: the CNOTs cancel and the control register is left untouched. The circuit really is UCR(α), not UCR(α) times some leftover entanglement.","title":"Why it works"},{"category":"section","location":"graycode/#Inverting-the-transform","page":"Gray coding","text":"Write the relation as α = M θ with M[j+1, i] = (-1)^(gray(i-1) · j). The rows of M are the characters of (Z₂)ᵏ evaluated at the points gray(i-1), which run over the whole group — so M is a column-permuted Walsh–Hadamard matrix and\n\nM Mᵀ = 2ᵏ I     ⟹     θ = Mᵀ α / 2ᵏ\n\nInverting a 2ᵏ × 2ᵏ system costs nothing but a transpose. In this package that is multiplex_angles:\n\njulia> using QuantumCircuits\n\njulia> c = multiplexed_rz([0.1, 0.5, -1.2, 2.0], [1, 2], 3)\nCircuit(3 qubits, 8 gates, 4 CNOTs)\nq1: ──────────────────────●──────────────────────●─\nq2: ──────────●───────────│──────────●───────────│─\nq3: ─RZ(0.35)─⊕─RZ(-0.90)─⊕─RZ(0.70)─⊕─RZ(-0.05)─⊕─\n\nFour branches, four CNOTs — and it stays four per branch as k grows, versus the ~2k CNOTs per branch a controlled-rotation-per-branch compilation would emit.\n\nNote the CNOT control pattern reading left to right: q2, q1, q2, q1 — the Gray flip sequence 0, 1, 0, 1 for k = 2, with the last step being the wrap-around that closes the cycle.","title":"Inverting the transform"},{"category":"section","location":"graycode/#4.-Diagonal-unitaries:-2ⁿ-2-CNOTs","page":"Gray coding","text":"diagonal(φ) builds diag(exp(i φ_j)). Split the basis index j = (j', b) on the last qubit and look at each pair of phases φ(j',0), φ(j',1):\n\ntheir difference is a relative phase between |0⟩ and |1⟩ of the last qubit, conditioned on j' — that is precisely a uniformly controlled RZ, one multiplexor, 2ⁿ⁻¹ CNOTs;\ntheir mean does not involve the last qubit at all — it is a diagonal unitary on n-1 qubits. Recurse.\n\nCNOTs(n) = CNOTs(n-1) + 2ⁿ⁻¹,  CNOTs(1) = 0    ⟹    CNOTs(n) = 2ⁿ - 2\n\nThe recursion bottoms out at a global phase, which Circuit tracks in global_phase so that matrix(c) is exactly diag(exp(i φ)) — not merely equal up to phase. That matters as soon as the diagonal appears inside a larger controlled block, where the \"unobservable\" global phase becomes an observable relative phase.","title":"4. Diagonal unitaries: 2ⁿ - 2 CNOTs"},{"category":"section","location":"graycode/#5.-State-preparation","page":"Gray coding","text":"prepare_state(a) maps |0…0⟩ to any normalised amplitude vector in two passes, both Gray-coded:\n\nMagnitudes. For each level ℓ = 1 … n, a uniformly controlled RY with controls 1 … ℓ-1 and target ℓ. The branch angle for prefix p is 2·atan(‖subtree p1‖, ‖subtree p0‖): split the amplitude mass of the subtree between the two children. This is a binary-tree walk over the amplitude vector, and level ℓ costs 2^(ℓ-1) CNOTs — 2ⁿ - 2 in total.\nPhases. One diagonal(angle.(a)), another 2ⁿ - 2 CNOTs.\n\nTotal 2ⁿ⁺¹ - 4 CNOTs, ancilla-free. (The theoretical lower bound is 2ⁿ - n - 1; the Gray-code construction is within a small constant factor of it and is what most compilers actually ship.)\n\njulia> c = prepare_state([1, 2, 3im, 4])\nCircuit(2 qubits, 10 gates, 4 CNOTs)\nq1: ─RY(2.30)──────────●──────────●─RZ(0.79)───────────●──────────●─\nq2: ──────────RY(2.03)─⊕─RY(0.18)─⊕──────────RZ(-0.79)─⊕─RZ(0.79)─⊕─\nglobal phase: 0.3927 rad\n\njulia> round.(statevector(c), digits=4)\n4-element Vector{ComplexF64}:\n  0.1826 + 0.0im\n  0.3651 - 0.0im\n    -0.0 + 0.5477im\n  0.7303 + 0.0im","title":"5. State preparation"},{"category":"section","location":"graycode/#6.-Where-else-Gray-coding-shows-up","page":"Gray coding","text":"Not yet implemented here, but the same lever:\n\nMulti-controlled gates without ancillas. Barenco et al. (1995), Lemma 7.5: Cⁿ(U) from 2ⁿ controlled-V gates where V^(2ⁿ⁻¹) = U, with the control patterns enumerated in Gray-code order so consecutive terms differ by one CNOT.\nTrotterisation. exp(i θ P) for a Pauli string P is a CNOT ladder onto a parity qubit, an RZ, and the ladder undone. Ordering the terms of a Hamiltonian so consecutive strings are Gray-adjacent lets the ladders partially cancel — the standard \"Pauli-string ordering\" optimisation.\nBinary-to-unary / Gray-code qubit encodings for simulating fermionic or bosonic modes, where the one-bit-difference property means a hop between adjacent occupation levels touches a single qubit.\nQuantum arithmetic and QROM/LUT loading, where an address register is swept in Gray order so the address decoder updates incrementally.","title":"6. Where else Gray coding shows up"},{"category":"section","location":"graycode/#References","page":"Gray coding","text":"M. Möttönen, J. J. Vartiainen, V. Bergholm, M. M. Salomaa, Transformation of quantum states using uniformly controlled rotations, quant-ph/0407010 (2004).\nV. Bergholm, J. J. Vartiainen, M. Möttönen, M. M. Salomaa, Quantum circuits with uniformly controlled one-qubit gates, PRA 71, 052330 (2005).\nA. Barenco et al., Elementary gates for quantum computation, PRA 52, 3457 (1995) — Lemma 7.5 for the Gray-code multi-controlled construction.\nA. Shende, S. Bullock, I. Markov, Synthesis of quantum-logic circuits, IEEE TCAD 25, 1000 (2006).","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"#QuantumCircuits.jl","page":"Home","text":"A small, dependency-free Julia package for building quantum circuits and decomposing structured unitaries into elementary gates.\n\nThe theme of this first release is Gray coding: order the computational basis so consecutive states differ in exactly one bit, and the CNOT ladders in a decomposition collapse to a single CNOT per step. The Gray coding page has the derivation and the reason it matters.","title":"QuantumCircuits.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"Only stdlib dependencies, so setup is instant:\n\njulia> using Pkg; Pkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/geekymode/QuantumCircuits.jl\")\njulia> using QuantumCircuits\n\nOr clone the repo and work inside it with julia --project=..","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"using QuantumCircuits\n\nc = Circuit(2)\npush!(c, H(), 1)\npush!(c, CNOT(), 1, 2)\nstatevector(c)        # (|00⟩ + |11⟩)/√2\nmatrix(c)             # the 4×4 unitary","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-it-buys-you","page":"Home","text":"Task naive Gray-code (this package)\nUniformly controlled rotation, k controls O(k 2^k) CNOTs 2^k\nDiagonal unitary on n qubits O(n 2^n) 2^n - 2\nArbitrary state preparation O(n 2^n) 2^n+1 - 4","title":"What it buys you"},{"category":"section","location":"#Uniformly-controlled-(multiplexed)-rotations","page":"Home","text":"\"Rotate the target by α[j+1] when the controls read j\", in 2^k rotations and 2^k CNOTs, with no multi-controlled gates and no ancillas:\n\njulia> multiplexed_rz([0.1, 0.5, -1.2, 2.0], [1, 2], 3)\nCircuit(3 qubits, 8 gates, 4 CNOTs)\nq1: ──────────────────────●──────────────────────●─\nq2: ──────────●───────────│──────────●───────────│─\nq3: ─RZ(0.35)─⊕─RZ(-0.90)─⊕─RZ(0.70)─⊕─RZ(-0.05)─⊕─\n\nThe CNOT controls follow the Gray flip sequence; the rotation angles come from a Walsh–Hadamard transform of the branch angles (multiplex_angles).","title":"Uniformly controlled (multiplexed) rotations"},{"category":"section","location":"#Diagonal-unitaries","page":"Home","text":"c = diagonal([0.0, 0.3, 1.1, -0.7])                  # diag(exp(im*φ))\nmatrix(c) ≈ Diagonal(cis.([0.0, 0.3, 1.1, -0.7]))    # true, global phase included","title":"Diagonal unitaries"},{"category":"section","location":"#State-preparation","page":"Home","text":"julia> c = prepare_state([1, 2, 3im, 4])\nCircuit(2 qubits, 10 gates, 4 CNOTs)\nq1: ─RY(2.30)──────────●──────────●─RZ(0.79)───────────●──────────●─\nq2: ──────────RY(2.03)─⊕─RY(0.18)─⊕──────────RZ(-0.79)─⊕─RZ(0.79)─⊕─\nglobal phase: 0.3927 rad\n\njulia> statevector(c) ≈ normalize([1, 2, 3im, 4])\ntrue","title":"State preparation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Conventions","page":"Home","text":"Qubit 1 is the most significant bit of a basis index: q_1 q_2 dots q_nrangle has index sum_q b_q 2^n-q. The top wire in a drawing is the leftmost bit in a ket.\ncontrols[1] is the most significant bit of a multiplexor's branch index j, matching the same reading order.\nRotations are R(theta) = exp(-itheta P2).\nCircuits carry an explicit global_phase, so matrix and statevector are exact rather than correct-up-to-phase — which is what you need once a block is used as the target of a control.","title":"Conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"#Running-the-examples-and-tests","page":"Home","text":"julia --project=. examples/demo.jl\njulia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'","title":"Running the examples and tests"}]
}
